DEVLOG.md:83:DID: re-read the Mutation 2 material end to end rather than working from the summary — §8's preamble on the three accumulating contracts, the companion discussion of per-allocation overhead and slabs (pp. 10–11, incl. the `kmem_cache`/Solaris lineage), §8.2's API and constraints, Figure 4 and its caption, the "what still comes from `rb_malloc`" paragraph, the ASan/poisoning discussion, the M2 rubric row, and the spec's own adversarial-review prompt for M2. Worked through what an `rb_pool_t` owns (slabs, a carve cursor, a free-list head), the three states a slot can be in (live / free-listed / uncarved), why the intrusive `next` may legally live in a dead object's own bytes (the slot is dead, big enough, and — given the stride — aligned enough), why `pool_alloc`/`pool_free` are O(1) (all deciding happened at `pool_create`; no size search, no coalescing, and no object->slab lookup on the hot path), and why `pool_destroy` can release whole slabs with live objects in them (the pool owns slabs, not objects). Then laid out the three decisions the spec leaves open, with tradeoffs and failure paths for each, and did not choose.
DEVLOG.md:84:DECIDED: (1) slots are aligned to `_Alignof(max_align_t)`, with the stride `obj_size` rounded up to that alignment and never below `sizeof(void *)`, so every slot address is aligned rather than only slot 0 — the pool cannot be told its caller's alignment (`pool_create` takes only `obj_size`), so it promises one conservatively; noted explicitly that this is fundamental alignment and NOT support for over-aligned (`_Alignas`-beyond-`max_align_t`) types. (2) slab metadata lives in-band at the front of each slab, accepting the payload cost and the "header eats bytes the naive division forgets" off-by-one in exchange for one `rb_malloc` per slab and therefore one failure point when the pool grows, instead of out-of-band's second allocation with its own unwind path. (3) `free_objs` follows the spec's operational definition — free-list slots plus uncarved capacity in existing slabs — giving `live + free_objs == slabs * objs_per_slab`. Full reasoning, costs accepted, and consequences recorded in NOTES.md under HW2 "Choices left up to me".
DEVLOG.md:87:OPEN: how the three `pool_stats` numbers are computed (maintained counters vs. an on-demand free-list walk vs. counters plus a debug-only cross-check) and how tests reach `objs_per_slab` without recomputing the geometry they are meant to check; whether `pool_create` buys a slab eagerly or lazily; whether `pool_stats` accepts NULL out-parameters; the degenerate oversized-`obj_size` case; the 0xDD debug poisoning the rubric requires and its conflict with the free-list link over the same bytes; and the `kmem_cache` comparison paragraph owed to REFLECTION.md.
DEVLOG.md:91:DID: added `rb_pool_geometry(obj_size, &stride, &slot0_offset, &objs_per_slab, &slack)` and the in-band `struct rb_slab` header to `src/pool.c`; added `test_pool_geometry_table()` to `tests/test_rbtree.c` (20 rows, ~120 assertions) and wired `src/pool.c` into the Makefile's `SRC`. Verified the test is load-bearing rather than vacuous by compiling three mutated scratch copies of `pool.c` against the unmodified test: dividing 4096 instead of the post-header usable span failed 34 assertions, dropping the alignment rounding failed 22, and handing out the tail slack as a partial slot (ceiling instead of floor division) failed 12. Full battery green: `make test` 770 assertions + 100,000 fuzz ops, `make asan` (address+undefined) clean, `make memcheck` clean from a clean rebuild — 605/605 allocs/frees on the unit binary, 20,152/20,152 on the fuzzer, 0 errors.
DEVLOG.md:92:DECIDED: (1) no `src/pool.h` in this slice. The spec's file-level map (§7) names only `src/pool.c`, and the repo already has the `RBTREE_TEST_HOOKS` precedent for test-only visibility into internals, so the geometry type and helper stay inside `src/pool.c` and the test declares the helper itself. Out-parameters instead of a shared geometry struct follow directly: with no header, a struct would have to be duplicated in the test, and a duplicated layout is a real hazard for no benefit. The helper has external linkage rather than `static` specifically so the production build (where no `pool_create` calls it yet) stays clean under `-Wall -Wextra -Werror` without broadening `RBTREE_TEST_HOOKS` or adding build machinery — a `static` unused function would have been a `-Wunused-function` error. Both builds were compiled separately to confirm. (2) internal degenerate-size contract: `obj_size == 0` returns false, any size for which no complete slot fits returns false, the oversize check runs *before* the alignment rounding so the arithmetic can never wrap (hence `SIZE_MAX` returns false rather than reporting capacity that does not exist), and the output geometry is zeroed on every false return. This is explicitly an internal contract — what `pool_create` should do with a rejected `obj_size` stays a later decision. (3) the direct test asserts the partition as `slot0_offset + objs_per_slab*stride + slack == 4096`, and tests minimal padding without overflow via `effective_min = max(obj_size, sizeof(void *))`, `stride >= effective_min`, `stride - effective_min < ALIGN`. The table's expected numbers stay independently worked out by hand rather than recomputed from the production formula, since a test that re-derives the geometry it checks lets a geometry bug cancel out of its own check; a `static_assert` pins the ABI (`alignof(max_align_t) == 16`, `sizeof(void *) == 8`) those literals were worked out for, so another ABI fails loudly instead of silently unchecking them.
NOTES.md:409:slot stride is `obj_size` rounded up to that alignment, and is also never smaller than
NOTES.md:416:Why: `pool_create(size_t obj_size)` takes no alignment parameter, so the pool cannot be told
NOTES.md:418:promise costs some internal padding when `obj_size` is not already a multiple of it — for the
NOTES.md:479:or lazily; and what a `obj_size` too large to fit even once should do.
