DEVLOG.md:83:DID: re-read the Mutation 2 material end to end rather than working from the summary — §8's preamble on the three accumulating contracts, the companion discussion of per-allocation overhead and slabs (pp. 10–11, incl. the `kmem_cache`/Solaris lineage), §8.2's API and constraints, Figure 4 and its caption, the "what still comes from `rb_malloc`" paragraph, the ASan/poisoning discussion, the M2 rubric row, and the spec's own adversarial-review prompt for M2. Worked through what an `rb_pool_t` owns (slabs, a carve cursor, a free-list head), the three states a slot can be in (live / free-listed / uncarved), why the intrusive `next` may legally live in a dead object's own bytes (the slot is dead, big enough, and — given the stride — aligned enough), why `pool_alloc`/`pool_free` are O(1) (all deciding happened at `pool_create`; no size search, no coalescing, and no object->slab lookup on the hot path), and why `pool_destroy` can release whole slabs with live objects in them (the pool owns slabs, not objects). Then laid out the three decisions the spec leaves open, with tradeoffs and failure paths for each, and did not choose.
DEVLOG.md:84:DECIDED: (1) slots are aligned to `_Alignof(max_align_t)`, with the stride `obj_size` rounded up to that alignment and never below `sizeof(void *)`, so every slot address is aligned rather than only slot 0 — the pool cannot be told its caller's alignment (`pool_create` takes only `obj_size`), so it promises one conservatively; noted explicitly that this is fundamental alignment and NOT support for over-aligned (`_Alignas`-beyond-`max_align_t`) types. (2) slab metadata lives in-band at the front of each slab, accepting the payload cost and the "header eats bytes the naive division forgets" off-by-one in exchange for one `rb_malloc` per slab and therefore one failure point when the pool grows, instead of out-of-band's second allocation with its own unwind path. (3) `free_objs` follows the spec's operational definition — free-list slots plus uncarved capacity in existing slabs — giving `live + free_objs == slabs * objs_per_slab`. Full reasoning, costs accepted, and consequences recorded in NOTES.md under HW2 "Choices left up to me".
